deep.reconstruction:
Context. In the 1986 'Analysis situs (deuxième mouture)' notes, Grothendieck builds an axiomatic, lattice-theoretic framework for tame, stratified 'figures'. The ingredients are:
- a class 𝓕 of figures and a subclass 𝓛 of irreducible or elementary figures (strata or cells);
- an order ≤ ('is a subfigure of');
- a finer relation F' ≪ F, read as 'F' is a union of cells of some refinement of the stratification of F';
- a symmetric relation ∥, apparently 'disjoint' or 'without interaction', with its negation \overline{∥} ('meets');
- for each figure, a set of strata or elements amb(F), alternatively written comb(F);
- numbered axioms At 1–At 13.
At 13 (p. 83) says the following. Let F ≪ X with X ∈ 𝓜. If some nonempty G ∈ 𝓕 satisfies G ∥ F and G \overline{∥} X, then some stratum x of X satisfies x ∥ F. In Grothendieck's own margin words this is an 'axiom of existence of many elements' or 'completeness'.

Corollary on pp. 83–84. Let F' ≪ F. The following are equivalent:
(i) every figure G disjoint from F' is disjoint from F (hence G∥F' ⇔ G∥F);
(ii) every stratum x of F meets F';
(iii) F' is maximal, for ≤, among the figures \overline{F'} with F' ≤ \overline{F'} ≪ F. That is, F' is maximal among the '≪-minorants' of F.
A crossed-out (iv) asked for maximality with respect to ≪ instead of ≤.

Proof as written.
- (i) ⇔ (ii) follows from the preceding proposition together with At 13 and the remark after it.
- (iii) ⇒ (ii), by contraposition. Suppose a stratum x of F is disjoint from F'. Put \overline{F'} = F' ∨ x. Then \overline{F'} ≪ F, checked stratum by stratum using At 4 (≪ is local on strata). Since F' < \overline{F'} strictly, (iii) fails.
- (ii) ⇒ (iii). Suppose F' ≤ \overline{F'} ≪ F with F' ≠ \overline{F'}. Take a cell x of \overline{F'} that is not a cell of F'. Then x ≪ \overline{F'} ≪ F gives x ≪ F, and x ∥ F'. This contradicts (ii).
The page ends with the open question 'Mais impliquent-ils (iv)?'.

On p. 85 these conditions define F' ≼ F: 'F' is a subdivision of F', or 'F is a regrouping of F''. Grothendieck then proves that ≼ is an order, that it is stable under restriction, and that it is local on coverings.

Modern reading: an axiomatic characterization of 'refinement or subdivision of a stratified (tame) space'. There are three equivalent forms: an external one (disjointness test against all figures), an internal one (covers every stratum), and an extremal one (maximal partial refinement).
